## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a two-level concurrency control that scales, maintains per-shard ordering, and exposes backpressure and fairness under bursty traffic.

## Key Concepts

- Bounded global queue with per‑shard FIFO queues
- Backpressure strategies and producer throttling
- Fair scheduling across shards (weighted RR or token buckets)
- Graceful shutdown, error propagation, testability

## Code Example

```rust
use std::sync::mpsc::{sync_channel, SyncSender, Receiver};
use std::thread;

fn main() {
  // Global bounded queue: (shard_id, value)
  let (global_tx, global_rx) = sync_channel::<(usize, i32)>(8);

  // Per-shard queues
  let (shard1_tx, shard1_rx) = sync_channel::<i32>(4);
  let (shard2_tx, shard2_rx) = sync_channel::<i32>(4);

  // Dispatcher: routes by shard
  thread::spawn(move || {
    while let Ok((sid, v)) = global_rx.recv() {
      if sid == 1 { shard1_tx.send(v).unwrap(); }
      else { shard2_tx.send(v).unwrap(); }
    }
  });

  // Simple workers: pull from shard queues
  let _w1 = thread::spawn(move || {
    while let Ok(x) = shard1_rx.recv() { /* process x preserving shard1 order */ }
  });
  let _w2 = thread::spawn(move || {
    while let Ok(x) = shard2_rx.recv() { /* process x preserving shard2 order */ }
  });

  // enqueue example
  global_tx.send((1, 123)).unwrap();
  global_tx.send((2, 456)).unwrap();

  // graceful shutdown omitted for brevity
}
```

